sentences.l:
Yang Huo wanted to see Kongzi;
Kongzi would not see him.
He sent Kongzi a young pig.
Kongzi, timing it for when he was out, went to bow his thanks to him;
he met him on the road.

The Master said:
“In nature, near to one another;
in habit, far from one another.”

Gongshan Furao rebelled, holding Bi;
he summoned (the Master),
and the Master wanted to go.

Zilu was not pleased and said:
“Nowhere to go — then that is all;
why must it be the Gongshan man you go to?”

Zizhang asked Kongzi about humaneness.
Kongzi said: “To be able to practise five things everywhere under Heaven — that is humaneness.”
“May I ask what they are?”
He said: “Respect, breadth of mind, good faith, quickness, kindness.
Respectful, and you are not slighted;
broad-minded, and you win the many;
true to your word, and others rely on you;
quick, and you achieve things;
kind, and you are able to employ others.”

Fond of humaneness, not fond of learning: its clouding is folly;
fond of wisdom, not fond of learning: its clouding is drifting;
fond of good faith, not fond of learning: its clouding is doing harm;
fond of straightness, not fond of learning: its clouding is harshness;
fond of courage, not fond of learning: its clouding is unruliness;
fond of firmness, not fond of learning: its clouding is recklessness.

The Master said:
“In old times the people had three failings;
now perhaps even these are lacking.”

The wild of old were unconstrained;
the wild of today are dissolute.
The stiff of old were sharp-edged;
the stiff of today are quarrelsome and contrary.
The simple of old were straight;
the simple of today are deceitful, and that is all.

The Master said:
“I hate purple robbing vermilion;
I hate the sounds of Zheng throwing proper music into disorder;
I hate sharp mouths that overturn states and houses.”

Ru Bei wished to see Kongzi;
Kongzi declined on the grounds of illness.
The one carrying the message went out the door;
(Kongzi) took up the zither and sang, to make him hear it.

If the gentleman for three years does not practise the rites,
the rites will surely fall to ruin;
if for three years he does not practise music,
music will surely collapse.

Zigong said: “Does the gentleman too have things he hates?”
The Master said:
“He has things he hates:
he hates those who tell of the bad in others;
hates those who live low down the stream and slander those above;
hates those brave but without the rites;
hates those resolute and daring but blocked.”

He said: “Ci, do you too have things you hate?” —
“I hate those who pry and take it for wisdom;
hate those who are not humble and take it for courage;
hate those who expose others and take it for straightness.”
